1
00:00:00,089 --> 00:00:01,589
<i>Previously on</i> Reacher...

2
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:03,091
[Foster] <i>No one knows
the three of you are here.</i>

3
00:00:03,092 --> 00:00:05,385
We are treating you
like enemy combatants on U.S. soil.

4
00:00:05,386 --> 00:00:06,803
We can do whatever we want.

5
00:00:06,804 --> 00:00:09,889
Where is the drive that Anna Merrick gave
to you on the train?

6
00:00:09,890 --> 00:00:13,059
[grunting]

7
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:15,645
[dramatic music playing]

8
00:00:15,646 --> 00:00:17,439
- Get in!
- [Reacher] <i>Who are you?</i>

9
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:20,567
Russell Plum, freelance journalist.

10
00:00:20,568 --> 00:00:22,235
- [Tamara] What is all this?
- [Russell] My research.

11
00:00:22,236 --> 00:00:24,320
I've been investigating the CIA for years.

12
00:00:24,321 --> 00:00:27,198
In the '90s, my mother met
a U.S. soldier named John.

13
00:00:27,199 --> 00:00:29,576
<i>Congressman John Sampson.</i>

14
00:00:29,577 --> 00:00:32,454
They had an affair, and you're basically
walking with the result of that union.

15
00:00:32,455 --> 00:00:34,456
My doctors say
I need a bone marrow transplant,

16
00:00:34,457 --> 00:00:36,166
but matches are hard to come by.

17
00:00:36,167 --> 00:00:38,418
You were in country
when my source was conceived.

18
00:00:38,419 --> 00:00:39,586
You think I had an affair?

19
00:00:39,587 --> 00:00:42,757
If my DNA's all it's gonna take
to get you to leave us alone...

20
00:00:43,799 --> 00:00:45,258
...here you go.

21
00:00:45,259 --> 00:00:46,677
[Tamara] Someone was looking
for the drive.

22
00:00:47,845 --> 00:00:50,847
- Your nephew?
- Yeah, I wish he'd call me back.

23
00:00:50,848 --> 00:00:52,432
Hey, who's that guy talking to Sampson?

24
00:00:52,433 --> 00:00:55,101
[paparazzo] That's Nolan Cahill,
Sampson's chief of staff.

25
00:00:55,102 --> 00:00:56,603
Remember when I said
there was a fourth witness

26
00:00:56,604 --> 00:00:59,482
on the train when Anna killed herself?
That's him.

27
00:01:10,284 --> 00:01:12,286
[siren wails in distance]

28
00:01:13,954 --> 00:01:15,956
[♪ Squeeze playing "Tempted"]

29
00:01:17,124 --> 00:01:18,708
[Russell] You're back.

30
00:01:18,709 --> 00:01:20,668
Van's downstairs.

31
00:01:20,669 --> 00:01:22,879
- Here.
- What's this?

32
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:24,297
A cat.

33
00:01:24,298 --> 00:01:26,800
We need all the information
you can get on Nolan Cahill.

34
00:01:26,801 --> 00:01:28,843
He's John Sampson's chief of staff.

35
00:01:28,844 --> 00:01:30,136
Why? What's going on?

36
00:01:30,137 --> 00:01:31,847
My sister, uh...

37
00:01:33,432 --> 00:01:35,642
...Anna, she, um...

38
00:01:35,643 --> 00:01:38,019
The one you were talking about
in the van?

39
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:40,230
Works for the Pentagon,
had some kind of jump drive?

40
00:01:40,231 --> 00:01:41,940
She shot herself
in the head in front of me

41
00:01:41,941 --> 00:01:43,525
two nights ago on the Broad Street Line.

42
00:01:43,526 --> 00:01:44,901
[cat mews]

43
00:01:44,902 --> 00:01:47,028
<i>♪ laundry on the hill ♪</i>

44
00:01:47,029 --> 00:01:49,405
<i>♪ billboards and the buildings ♪</i>

45
00:01:49,406 --> 00:01:52,408
<i>♪ Memories of it still keep calling ♪</i>

46
00:01:52,409 --> 00:01:53,785
A little tact?

47
00:01:53,786 --> 00:01:55,745
<i>♪ and calling...</i>

48
00:01:55,746 --> 00:01:57,705
Cahill was on the train
the night she died.

49
00:01:57,706 --> 00:01:58,998
We need to know why.

50
00:01:58,999 --> 00:02:00,875
A woman kills herself on the same train

51
00:02:00,876 --> 00:02:03,336
as the chief of staff
of a potential presidential candidate.

52
00:02:03,337 --> 00:02:05,797
The CIA starts kidnapping people over it.

53
00:02:05,798 --> 00:02:07,549
It-It's a tragedy,

54
00:02:07,550 --> 00:02:11,095
clearly, of course, but that's a story.

55
00:02:12,054 --> 00:02:13,680
Give me a beat,
I'll get into some records.

56
00:02:13,681 --> 00:02:17,392
<i>You got Ben. Leave a message, or live like
you're in the 21st century and text me.</i>

57
00:02:17,393 --> 00:02:18,351
<i>[beeps]</i>

58
00:02:18,352 --> 00:02:20,019
[female voice] <i>The mailbox is full</i>

59
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:22,356
<i>- and cannot--</i>
- Fuck!

60
00:02:23,649 --> 00:02:25,441
<i>♪ another ♪</i>

61
00:02:25,442 --> 00:02:28,611
<i>♪ Tempted, but the truth is discovered...</i>

62
00:02:28,612 --> 00:02:30,030
I got it.

63
00:02:31,156 --> 00:02:32,283
I got it. Sorry.

64
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:34,993
He's a liability.

65
00:02:34,994 --> 00:02:36,578
Inexperienced, all emotion.

66
00:02:36,579 --> 00:02:38,538
- He's a cop.
- From Mayberry.

67
00:02:38,539 --> 00:02:40,248
- Mayfield.
- Same thing.

68
00:02:40,249 --> 00:02:43,126
We don't know what we're up against.
We need all the help we can find.

69
00:02:43,127 --> 00:02:44,794
- He can't help us.
- How do you know?

70
00:02:44,795 --> 00:02:47,505
You ever have a sibling get killed
over some fucked-up illegal shit?

71
00:02:47,506 --> 00:02:49,132
Yes.

72
00:02:49,133 --> 00:02:50,675
- What?
- Bottom line?

73
00:02:50,676 --> 00:02:53,637
He doesn't pull it together,
I'll leave him on the side of the road.

74
00:02:54,638 --> 00:02:56,055
Who are you calling?

75
00:02:56,056 --> 00:02:59,183
Lila. Sampson's being straight
about not being her father.

76
00:02:59,184 --> 00:03:02,061
Jumped at the chance to provide DNA.

77
00:03:02,062 --> 00:03:04,188
I want to know why she lied to me
and what she really knows.

78
00:03:04,189 --> 00:03:06,275
Okay, found a lot.

79
00:03:07,902 --> 00:03:10,445
Cahill served
on the same Delta Team as Sampson.

80
00:03:10,446 --> 00:03:12,071
He was Sampson's commanding officer. Huh.

81
00:03:12,072 --> 00:03:14,407
He's got more medals than the Olympics.

82
00:03:14,408 --> 00:03:16,117
- Man like that knows a lot.
- [Jacob] Great.

83
00:03:16,118 --> 00:03:18,286
Now what are we doing here?
Let's go and confront him.

84
00:03:18,287 --> 00:03:20,580
- He won't talk.
- Then we make him talk.

85
00:03:20,581 --> 00:03:23,625
We can't pull a congressman's chief
of staff off the street and rough him up.

86
00:03:23,626 --> 00:03:25,752
That girl was sliced to pieces.

87
00:03:25,753 --> 00:03:27,879
I want my fuckin' nephew back!

88
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:30,340
Jacob, we're not fighting you. It's just--

89
00:03:30,341 --> 00:03:32,216
- [alarm sounding]
- Someone's outside.

90
00:03:32,217 --> 00:03:33,801
[alarm stops]

91
00:03:33,802 --> 00:03:36,096
[dramatic, eerie music playing]

92
00:03:37,514 --> 00:03:39,474
[Reacher] It's the CIA backup squad
from the black site.

93
00:03:39,475 --> 00:03:42,518
- How'd they find us?
- Oh, shit.

94
00:03:42,519 --> 00:03:43,979
[Reacher] What'd you do?

95
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:47,523
After you guys ditched me,
I may have posted something on 4Chan

96
00:03:47,524 --> 00:03:50,193
saying I found a thumb drive
on the Philly subway.

97
00:03:50,194 --> 00:03:54,197
It had Anna Merrick's name on the side
and military records on it.

98
00:03:54,198 --> 00:03:56,616
I asked if anyone had any intel,
maybe they should just reach out.

99
00:03:56,617 --> 00:03:57,700
What the fuck?

100
00:03:57,701 --> 00:03:59,661
You guys wouldn't tell me anything,
and you stole my van.

101
00:03:59,662 --> 00:04:01,621
All right? This story could give me
my career back.

102
00:04:01,622 --> 00:04:03,998
I figured I could flush out someone
who might know something.

103
00:04:03,999 --> 00:04:06,668
I used a VPN, I used an encryption
so they couldn't trace it.

104
00:04:06,669 --> 00:04:07,586
Looks like someone could.

105
00:04:08,754 --> 00:04:10,838
[Reacher] Maybe two someones.

106
00:04:10,839 --> 00:04:14,175
[Russell] Yeah, looks like
we got another car full of thugs.

107
00:04:14,176 --> 00:04:16,552
[Reacher] That one with the bruise was
at Lila's hotel yesterday.

108
00:04:16,553 --> 00:04:18,388
Who the hell are these guys?

109
00:04:18,389 --> 00:04:19,681
Doesn't matter
if they're private contractors,

110
00:04:19,682 --> 00:04:21,683
federal boys or the '75 Cincinnati Reds.

111
00:04:21,684 --> 00:04:22,976
If they're here, it's not good.

112
00:04:22,977 --> 00:04:25,353
Think the Big Red Machine's working
with the CIA as ghost ops?

113
00:04:25,354 --> 00:04:26,522
Maybe.

114
00:04:29,566 --> 00:04:31,234
I don't think they're working together.

115
00:04:31,235 --> 00:04:32,443
They're coming in.

116
00:04:32,444 --> 00:04:34,070
[powering down]

117
00:04:34,071 --> 00:04:35,905
Oh, shit. They cut the power.

118
00:04:35,906 --> 00:04:37,323
They'll use a standard protocol search,

119
00:04:37,324 --> 00:04:38,992
work their way up floor by floor
until they find us.

120
00:04:38,993 --> 00:04:40,493
[Russell] There's only one set of stairs.

121
00:04:40,494 --> 00:04:42,245
- We'll run straight into 'em.
- [Jacob] Is there any way off the roof?

122
00:04:42,246 --> 00:04:43,955
- Or a fire escape?
- No.

123
00:04:43,956 --> 00:04:46,332
- Where are your knives?
- I don't have any knives. I eat take-out.

124
00:04:46,333 --> 00:04:49,085
- You got any weapons?
- I have five cans of wasp spray.

125
00:04:49,086 --> 00:04:50,586
- Why?
- I have wasps.

126
00:04:50,587 --> 00:04:52,840
Get 'em.
Jacob, grab the fire extinguisher.

127
00:04:55,509 --> 00:04:58,344
- [mewing]
- Stay here.

128
00:04:58,345 --> 00:05:01,515
Use that to create cover so Tamara can
start shooting when she hears the signal.

129
00:05:03,726 --> 00:05:05,060
What's the signal?

130
00:05:06,103 --> 00:05:07,146
Screaming.

131
00:05:08,147 --> 00:05:10,149
[thunder rumbling]

132
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:23,162
[tense, dramatic music playing]

133
00:05:30,919 --> 00:05:32,880
[Jacob] Come on, hurry. They're coming.

134
00:05:36,675 --> 00:05:39,010
[Reacher grunts]

135
00:05:39,011 --> 00:05:41,013
[panting]

136
00:05:50,814 --> 00:05:52,065
[grunts]

137
00:05:52,066 --> 00:05:54,902
[groaning]

138
00:06:06,663 --> 00:06:07,790
[man] Clear!

139
00:06:14,171 --> 00:06:15,713
- [Reacher grunts softly]
- [man 2] Clear.

140
00:06:15,714 --> 00:06:17,716
[groaning]

141
00:06:22,679 --> 00:06:25,640
- Keep it down.
- I can't find the fucking wasp spray.

142
00:06:25,641 --> 00:06:28,185
[strained groaning]

143
00:06:31,188 --> 00:06:32,523
[grunts]

144
00:06:38,028 --> 00:06:40,030
[panting]

145
00:06:43,992 --> 00:06:45,994
[slow, tense music playing]

146
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:55,963
[footsteps approaching]

147
00:07:10,811 --> 00:07:12,813
[breathing heavily]

148
00:07:24,616 --> 00:07:26,200
- [grunting]
- [man yelling]

149
00:07:26,201 --> 00:07:27,577
Now!

150
00:07:27,578 --> 00:07:29,287
- [gunfire]
- [yelling]

151
00:07:29,288 --> 00:07:32,415
[gunfire continues]

152
00:07:32,416 --> 00:07:34,250
[yelling]

153
00:07:34,251 --> 00:07:35,585
- [gunshot]
- [grunts]

154
00:07:35,586 --> 00:07:39,298
[grunting]

155
00:07:45,179 --> 00:07:47,181
[panting]

156
00:07:50,934 --> 00:07:53,270
[man panting]

157
00:08:01,195 --> 00:08:03,238
Still alive? Good.

158
00:08:04,573 --> 00:08:05,782
[grunting]

159
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:10,162
[head continues clanging on steps]

160
00:08:14,249 --> 00:08:15,833
- [grunts]
- Who do you work for?

161
00:08:15,834 --> 00:08:18,127
- I need a doctor.
- You want a doctor?

162
00:08:18,128 --> 00:08:20,213
- I want to know who hired you.
- Please.

163
00:08:20,214 --> 00:08:22,548
- Hey, tell me where Ben is right now!
- Hey. Get him out of here!

164
00:08:22,549 --> 00:08:25,092
- [grunts]
- You have to stay calm.

165
00:08:25,093 --> 00:08:26,969
Stay calm. He knows where Ben is.

166
00:08:26,970 --> 00:08:28,472
But he can't say shit if you kill him.

167
00:08:29,473 --> 00:08:31,725
We need that man alive
if we're gonna get intel.

168
00:08:34,311 --> 00:08:37,564
Uh... that's not gonna happen.

169
00:08:39,483 --> 00:08:42,361
- You're robbing him?
- We need to figure out who these guys are.

170
00:08:44,029 --> 00:08:46,280
Their guns could be traced back
to murdered CIA.

171
00:08:46,281 --> 00:08:48,783
- Oh, shit.
- What is it?

172
00:08:48,784 --> 00:08:52,536
Two addresses. One's this place,
one's hopefully not what I think it is.

173
00:08:52,537 --> 00:08:54,956
[Tamara] All right, we got to move, guys.

174
00:08:54,957 --> 00:08:56,332
[line ringing]

175
00:08:56,333 --> 00:08:57,875
Lila, it's Reacher.

176
00:08:57,876 --> 00:08:59,877
Your pal from the hotel yesterday
paid another visit.

177
00:08:59,878 --> 00:09:02,088
He's not a problem anymore,
but he had the address

178
00:09:02,089 --> 00:09:03,839
to the Rittenhouse Hotel on him.

179
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:06,008
If that's where you are, leave now.

180
00:09:06,009 --> 00:09:07,260
I'll find you.

181
00:09:07,261 --> 00:09:08,678
[♪ Urban Dance Squad sings
"A Deeper Shade of Soul"]

182
00:09:08,679 --> 00:09:10,554
<i>♪ A deeper shade of soul, yeah ♪</i>

183
00:09:10,555 --> 00:09:14,809
- <i>♪ A deeper shade of soul ♪</i>
- <i>♪ Took a long time...</i>

184
00:09:14,810 --> 00:09:17,103
- [panting loudly]
- Hey?

185
00:09:17,104 --> 00:09:18,604
- What?
- You good?

186
00:09:18,605 --> 00:09:21,399
Yeah, man. I...
I just got to get out of D.C.

187
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:23,150
I mean, you have no idea
what this city can be like

188
00:09:23,151 --> 00:09:24,652
when they have you in their crosshairs.

189
00:09:24,653 --> 00:09:27,113
They killed my career.
Now they're gonna kill me.

190
00:09:27,114 --> 00:09:29,283
Can we please just go any faster?

191
00:09:30,284 --> 00:09:32,368
We need to make a stop in town first.

192
00:09:32,369 --> 00:09:33,911
- Wha...?
- What? Are you kidding me?

193
00:09:33,912 --> 00:09:35,371
We're on the run, and we need cash.

194
00:09:35,372 --> 00:09:37,290
Got to hit an ATM while we're in the city.

195
00:09:37,291 --> 00:09:38,833
The people that want us dead
know we're in D.C.

196
00:09:38,834 --> 00:09:41,210
By the time they get a bead on the ATM,
we're long gone.

197
00:09:41,211 --> 00:09:42,962
Yeah, man. Might be following us already.

198
00:09:42,963 --> 00:09:45,423
Before they came up to my loft,
they saw the van

199
00:09:45,424 --> 00:09:47,591
in the garage, they probably called it in
to their bosses.

200
00:09:47,592 --> 00:09:49,010
Our vehicle's been compromised.

201
00:09:49,011 --> 00:09:50,511
You got another skin
under the Plumber sign?

202
00:09:50,512 --> 00:09:52,847
Yeah, but they have the make and model.
We got to get a new ride.

203
00:09:52,848 --> 00:09:54,140
We're wanted by the CIA.

204
00:09:54,141 --> 00:09:56,852
We can't exactly go to Hertz
and give 'em our licenses.

205
00:09:57,853 --> 00:09:58,854
I know a place.

206
00:09:59,855 --> 00:10:01,189
We can be there in less than two hours.

207
00:10:02,190 --> 00:10:05,193
{\an8}[♪ A.S.M. featuring Sadat X plays
"Certified Organic"]

208
00:10:10,866 --> 00:10:13,200
<i>♪ You've not heard of us,
this is certified organic</i>...

209
00:10:13,201 --> 00:10:14,452
[man] Yo, Cyrus.

210
00:10:14,453 --> 00:10:15,995
<i>♪ On its way, don't panic...</i>

211
00:10:15,996 --> 00:10:18,831
[tool whirring]

212
00:10:18,832 --> 00:10:22,001
Ah, fuck this. Nah, nah, nah, nah.

213
00:10:22,002 --> 00:10:23,919
Me and Docherty cut a deal.

214
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:27,465
I get you dirt on those boys
from Norristown, you leave me alone.

215
00:10:27,466 --> 00:10:29,216
Docherty made that deal, not me.

216
00:10:29,217 --> 00:10:30,343
I don't make deals with punks.

217
00:10:30,344 --> 00:10:32,887
- Then what you doing here?
- I need to make a deal with some punks.

218
00:10:32,888 --> 00:10:34,138
And who are your white boys?

219
00:10:34,139 --> 00:10:36,515
[Tamara] My backup dancers.
Want to trade my Windstar

220
00:10:36,516 --> 00:10:37,767
for your Highlander?

221
00:10:37,768 --> 00:10:41,520
[laughs] That's money. That's bullshit.

222
00:10:41,521 --> 00:10:43,731
We both know that SUV's stolen.

223
00:10:43,732 --> 00:10:45,691
So, if you want,

224
00:10:45,692 --> 00:10:48,402
we can continue this conversation
at the District while I run the VIN.

225
00:10:48,403 --> 00:10:51,197
That is, if it isn't already filed off.

226
00:10:51,198 --> 00:10:54,076
If you were gonna bust us,
you'd have done it by now.

227
00:10:55,035 --> 00:10:56,535
You are absolutely right.

228
00:10:56,536 --> 00:10:59,622
Guess we'll have to figure out
another way to negotiate.

229
00:10:59,623 --> 00:11:01,207
<i>♪ I may not be the best alive,
but I count ♪</i>

230
00:11:01,208 --> 00:11:02,834
<i>♪ No matter the amount
of the trials I face</i>...

231
00:11:05,337 --> 00:11:06,671
[Cyrus] What are you doing?

232
00:11:07,631 --> 00:11:09,423
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

233
00:11:09,424 --> 00:11:12,468
Yo, that's 50 racks right there!
What you doing?

234
00:11:12,469 --> 00:11:14,637
What is she doing?

235
00:11:14,638 --> 00:11:16,138
Getting his attention.

236
00:11:16,139 --> 00:11:18,224
- Hey, yo--
- Let's try this again.

237
00:11:18,225 --> 00:11:19,809
My Windstar for your Highlander.

238
00:11:19,810 --> 00:11:22,895
'Cause in a few seconds, our only option
is to run our asses out of here.

239
00:11:22,896 --> 00:11:26,732
Shit. All right. All right.
All right, just put it out. Put it out.

240
00:11:26,733 --> 00:11:29,402
And throw in some plates
from a car that's properly registered.

241
00:11:29,403 --> 00:11:30,695
That Jetta?

242
00:11:31,780 --> 00:11:33,948
Nah, nah, nah.
That's my sister's car right there.

243
00:11:33,949 --> 00:11:36,118
Rayona'll kill me.
You know she's crazy, right?

244
00:11:36,952 --> 00:11:39,120
This rag is burning up pretty quickly.

245
00:11:39,121 --> 00:11:41,872
[tense, dramatic music playing]

246
00:11:41,873 --> 00:11:44,667
Okay. Okay. You know what?

247
00:11:44,668 --> 00:11:46,627
I think I can make it work with Ray Ray.

248
00:11:46,628 --> 00:11:49,171
I can figure it out.
Just please put it out. Put it out!

249
00:11:49,172 --> 00:11:51,174
[engine revving]

250
00:11:56,930 --> 00:11:58,931
[purring]

251
00:11:58,932 --> 00:12:00,975
- I'm-a call you Pancake.
- [mews]

252
00:12:00,976 --> 00:12:05,479
- [♪ The Coasters sing "Down in Mexico"]
<i>♪ Ah, ah ♪</i>

253
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:08,482
<i>♪ Down in the Mexicali ♪</i>

254
00:12:08,483 --> 00:12:09,859
<i>♪ Bop, doo-wop ♪</i>

255
00:12:09,860 --> 00:12:11,652
<i>♪ There's a crazy little place</i>...

256
00:12:11,653 --> 00:12:13,362
I liked that cat.

257
00:12:13,363 --> 00:12:14,780
- Ray Ray's all right.
- [phone ringing]

258
00:12:14,781 --> 00:12:16,324
Kitty's got a good home.

259
00:12:19,578 --> 00:12:22,163
[phone rings over speaker]

260
00:12:22,164 --> 00:12:23,664
Lila.

261
00:12:23,665 --> 00:12:25,374
[Lila] <i>Hey, I got your message.</i>

262
00:12:25,375 --> 00:12:27,751
Moved my family
to a new hotel immediately.

263
00:12:27,752 --> 00:12:30,796
We're settling in,
we're just a little freaked out.

264
00:12:30,797 --> 00:12:33,507
We need to talk. Where can you meet?

265
00:12:33,508 --> 00:12:34,717
<i>The park again?</i>

266
00:12:34,718 --> 00:12:36,051
We need someplace inside

267
00:12:36,052 --> 00:12:38,762
where the people looking for us
can't just see us driving by.

268
00:12:38,763 --> 00:12:41,557
A place that's crowded,
but quiet enough we can talk.

269
00:12:41,558 --> 00:12:43,058
<i>No loud bars.</i>

270
00:12:43,059 --> 00:12:45,936
I passed a bowling alley on the way here.

271
00:12:45,937 --> 00:12:47,605
<i>Playtime Bowl on 2nd.</i>

272
00:12:47,606 --> 00:12:50,191
Perfect. Head there now.
I'll see you soon.

273
00:12:50,192 --> 00:12:51,485
<i>Reacher?</i>

274
00:12:52,277 --> 00:12:53,694
[sighs]

275
00:12:53,695 --> 00:12:55,071
Thank you.

276
00:12:57,407 --> 00:13:00,534
I need to get some info
from Lila to confront Cahill with.

277
00:13:00,535 --> 00:13:02,870
He's a powerful guy with D.C. connections.

278
00:13:02,871 --> 00:13:05,206
I can't just show up
and try to intimidate him.

279
00:13:05,207 --> 00:13:07,209
Where do you want me
to drop you three off?

280
00:13:08,293 --> 00:13:09,543
You're going alone?

281
00:13:09,544 --> 00:13:12,087
What if more mercs are following Lila?
You'll need backup.

282
00:13:12,088 --> 00:13:13,672
Didn't need backup at the hotel.

283
00:13:13,673 --> 00:13:15,341
I was with you.

284
00:13:15,342 --> 00:13:16,800
Yeah, out cold in the hall.

285
00:13:16,801 --> 00:13:19,929
Odds are slim they'll find one woman
in this city that fast.

286
00:13:19,930 --> 00:13:21,890
Hopefully. Lila stands out.

287
00:13:22,974 --> 00:13:24,266
How so?

288
00:13:24,267 --> 00:13:25,977
She's what the kids today
call a smoke show.

289
00:13:27,521 --> 00:13:29,438
That why you want to meet her alone?

290
00:13:29,439 --> 00:13:31,524
I caught her in a lie.

291
00:13:31,525 --> 00:13:34,193
Can't just show up with two cops.
She's not gonna open her mouth.

292
00:13:34,194 --> 00:13:35,820
I have to go alone.

293
00:13:37,322 --> 00:13:38,782
Mm-hmm.

294
00:13:39,699 --> 00:13:42,076
Drop us off at the District for my shift.

295
00:13:42,077 --> 00:13:43,744
I'll check in with Docherty,
see what he found

296
00:13:43,745 --> 00:13:46,997
while you... probe Lila.

297
00:13:46,998 --> 00:13:49,208
<i>♪ I didn't know just what to expect ♪</i>

298
00:13:49,209 --> 00:13:50,543
- Hmm.
- <i>♪ in Mexico ♪</i>

299
00:13:50,544 --> 00:13:53,546
<i>♪ She threw her arms around my neck ♪</i>

300
00:13:53,547 --> 00:13:54,880
<i>♪ in Mexico ♪</i>

301
00:13:54,881 --> 00:13:57,258
<i>♪ We started dancing
all around the floor ♪</i>

302
00:13:57,259 --> 00:13:58,842
<i>♪ in Mexico ♪</i>

303
00:13:58,843 --> 00:14:02,263
<i>♪ and then she did a dance
I never saw before ♪</i>

304
00:14:02,264 --> 00:14:04,266
- [phones ringing]
- [indistinct chatter]

305
00:14:05,850 --> 00:14:07,560
[Docherty] Holy shit.

306
00:14:07,561 --> 00:14:11,063
It's Halley's comet,
coming by once every hundred years.

307
00:14:11,064 --> 00:14:13,399
It's 75, dickhead.
Where are we on the Merrick case?

308
00:14:13,400 --> 00:14:15,234
Look, fuck you. You were MIA again.

309
00:14:15,235 --> 00:14:17,069
It's becoming a habit.
I'm done helping you.

310
00:14:17,070 --> 00:14:19,446
I don't have time for this shit, man.
We're working something here.

311
00:14:19,447 --> 00:14:21,532
We? Who? You and the Fuck-It-Up Gang?

312
00:14:21,533 --> 00:14:24,910
The Feds took the case.
It's not our hot dog cart any more, Slick.

313
00:14:24,911 --> 00:14:27,454
That dead OnlyFans girl was found
in Philadelphia.

314
00:14:27,455 --> 00:14:29,707
That's our jurisdiction.
So what do we got?

315
00:14:29,708 --> 00:14:31,208
Or should I not give a shit
about homicides

316
00:14:31,209 --> 00:14:32,626
that happen in my own city anymore?

317
00:14:32,627 --> 00:14:34,670
- You're not a homicide detective.
- Neither are you.

318
00:14:34,671 --> 00:14:35,879
[chuckles]

319
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:37,424
What are you smiling at, Pee Wee?

320
00:14:38,508 --> 00:14:42,303
Look, whoever hired that girl, that escort
or whatever, they covered their tracks.

321
00:14:42,304 --> 00:14:44,555
VPN, offshore accounts. Untraceable.

322
00:14:44,556 --> 00:14:45,806
What about the ME?

323
00:14:45,807 --> 00:14:47,266
It's gonna be a few days on the report.

324
00:14:47,267 --> 00:14:48,351
Did you expedite it?

325
00:14:49,311 --> 00:14:51,020
- Yeah.
- And the guy in the library?

326
00:14:51,021 --> 00:14:53,689
The fucking tip line? Was that you?

327
00:14:53,690 --> 00:14:55,941
It doesn't matter who called it in.
What do we got on him?

328
00:14:55,942 --> 00:15:00,070
[exhales] His prints didn't match
anything in the girl's room.

329
00:15:00,071 --> 00:15:02,323
- And?
- You know what, guys?

330
00:15:02,324 --> 00:15:04,283
There's Tastykakes in the kitchen.

331
00:15:04,284 --> 00:15:06,620
- Cool. I could eat.
- I'm not hungry.

332
00:15:08,079 --> 00:15:10,957
Then grab some coffee. I got this.

333
00:15:15,253 --> 00:15:17,171
Ah, now what the hell are you doing?

334
00:15:17,172 --> 00:15:20,132
You already rat-fucked your career,
now you want to do it to mine?

335
00:15:20,133 --> 00:15:21,300
Got nothing to do with you.

336
00:15:21,301 --> 00:15:23,969
You know damn well
your shit splatters on me.

337
00:15:23,970 --> 00:15:26,722
I got a 40-year vest coming.
I ain't gonna let you shit it up.

338
00:15:26,723 --> 00:15:28,974
Big D, we found the Self Check-Out's car.

339
00:15:28,975 --> 00:15:31,310
- Near the subway stop?
- No.

340
00:15:31,311 --> 00:15:35,439
Merrick got on at Tasker-Morris.
Car was picked up at 6th and Carpenter.

341
00:15:35,440 --> 00:15:37,691
RPC's babysitting it.
You want to feed the Feds?

342
00:15:37,692 --> 00:15:39,109
It was your case.

343
00:15:39,110 --> 00:15:41,279
You tell 'em. I don't care.

344
00:15:43,448 --> 00:15:44,866
Copy that.

345
00:15:50,872 --> 00:15:53,582
- It's not our case anymore.
- Anna Merrick isn't.

346
00:15:53,583 --> 00:15:55,626
No one said
I couldn't look into a stolen car.

347
00:15:55,627 --> 00:15:57,920
Could be a stolen car ring here in Philly.

348
00:15:57,921 --> 00:15:59,421
You've been phoning it in for years.

349
00:15:59,422 --> 00:16:01,257
Now, all of a sudden,
you want to be a cop again?

350
00:16:02,175 --> 00:16:06,054
Have you forgotten what happened
the last time you couldn't let a case go?

351
00:16:09,265 --> 00:16:11,851
I'm getting Tastykakes.

352
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:16,438
A pain in my ass.

353
00:16:16,439 --> 00:16:18,483
We got the car.

354
00:16:19,693 --> 00:16:22,361
- [♪ Eddie Floyd sings "Knock on Wood"]
- <i>♪ I don't want to lose ♪</i>

355
00:16:22,362 --> 00:16:24,239
<i>♪ this good thing ♪</i>

356
00:16:26,408 --> 00:16:28,118
<i>♪ that I've got ♪</i>

357
00:16:28,827 --> 00:16:30,495
<i>♪ If I do ♪</i>

358
00:16:31,371 --> 00:16:33,831
<i>♪ I would surely...</i>

359
00:16:33,832 --> 00:16:36,291
Reacher, thank you again for warning us.

360
00:16:36,292 --> 00:16:38,628
It's the last thing I'll do for you
if you don't tell me the truth.

361
00:16:40,213 --> 00:16:42,089
You lied about Sampson.

362
00:16:42,090 --> 00:16:44,384
I don't believe he's your father,
and neither do you.

363
00:16:50,849 --> 00:16:52,349
Being honest is the least you can do,

364
00:16:52,350 --> 00:16:55,145
considering I confronted
a U.S. congressman with your bullshit.

365
00:17:01,943 --> 00:17:03,819
You're right.

366
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:05,779
I wasn't entirely truthful.

367
00:17:05,780 --> 00:17:07,406
I'm a journalist.

368
00:17:07,407 --> 00:17:10,744
Amisha is not my mother,
and these two are hired protection.

369
00:17:11,828 --> 00:17:13,203
Protecting you from what?

370
00:17:13,204 --> 00:17:16,290
I'm here to expose the secrets of
the most powerful government on earth.

371
00:17:16,291 --> 00:17:17,541
And the cancer?

372
00:17:17,542 --> 00:17:20,210
Made the bruises myself
so I could get to Sampson.

373
00:17:20,211 --> 00:17:21,795
So you could lie to him, too?

374
00:17:21,796 --> 00:17:25,007
Look, I'm truly sorry, okay?

375
00:17:25,008 --> 00:17:27,135
But you could've had information
we needed.

376
00:17:28,303 --> 00:17:29,970
And I was scared.

377
00:17:29,971 --> 00:17:33,056
A woman died. Men came after me.

378
00:17:33,057 --> 00:17:35,143
Tell me the real story.

379
00:17:39,439 --> 00:17:40,899
Fries are communal.

380
00:17:41,649 --> 00:17:42,733
Go on.

381
00:17:42,734 --> 00:17:46,612
Amisha and Sampson didn't have an affair,
but they are connected in another way.

382
00:17:46,613 --> 00:17:49,865
When Amisha and her husband
were still students in Indonesia,

383
00:17:49,866 --> 00:17:52,367
they were concerned
with the growing influence of the military

384
00:17:52,368 --> 00:17:54,328
under a man named General Putra.

385
00:17:54,329 --> 00:17:56,622
So, they had rallies.

386
00:17:56,623 --> 00:17:59,917
And soon after that, the whole
entire group was illegally incarcerated.

387
00:17:59,918 --> 00:18:02,211
Putra committed atrocities.

388
00:18:02,212 --> 00:18:04,505
Amisha listened
as her husband was brutally beaten

389
00:18:04,506 --> 00:18:06,590
and killed in a cell next to hers.

390
00:18:06,591 --> 00:18:08,842
And then she was beaten
within an inch of her own life.

391
00:18:08,843 --> 00:18:13,096
Putra and his soldiers were trained
by an American Delta Force specialist.

392
00:18:13,097 --> 00:18:14,264
John Sampson.

393
00:18:14,265 --> 00:18:16,642
Yeah, and that's why he was in Indonesia
in the first place.

394
00:18:16,643 --> 00:18:19,353
To teach Putra's soldiers
advanced military tactics.

395
00:18:19,354 --> 00:18:23,273
The very tactics that were used against
the civilians Putra tortured and killed.

396
00:18:23,274 --> 00:18:24,817
What about Nolan Cahill?

397
00:18:24,818 --> 00:18:28,278
He was... he was Sampson's
commanding officer in Indonesia. Why?

398
00:18:28,279 --> 00:18:30,072
He was on the train the night Anna died.

399
00:18:30,073 --> 00:18:32,116
♪ ♪

400
00:18:35,787 --> 00:18:39,081
He must've been there to stop Anna
from delivering the drive to me,

401
00:18:39,082 --> 00:18:41,083
getting the story to me.

402
00:18:41,084 --> 00:18:44,462
America wants to scrub its involvement
clean, but the public deserves to know.

403
00:18:45,463 --> 00:18:46,797
After sitting with Amisha,

404
00:18:46,798 --> 00:18:49,758
listening to her story,
looking in her eyes, at her face,

405
00:18:49,759 --> 00:18:53,428
seeing what Putra did to her,
Anna Merrick agreed to help.

406
00:18:53,429 --> 00:18:54,972
And that's why
she was bringing me that drive,

407
00:18:54,973 --> 00:18:56,807
'cause she appreciated what's at stake.

408
00:18:56,808 --> 00:19:00,061
Bet she appreciated stealing
government records is a federal crime.

409
00:19:01,145 --> 00:19:03,939
Anna chose to work for the Pentagon
'cause she's a patriot.

410
00:19:03,940 --> 00:19:06,608
And she, like I,

411
00:19:06,609 --> 00:19:09,361
believed a man like Sampson,
a man with his morals,

412
00:19:09,362 --> 00:19:11,698
cannot take the White House.

413
00:19:12,699 --> 00:19:14,325
I have to get this story out.

414
00:19:20,039 --> 00:19:21,249
Please.

415
00:19:22,250 --> 00:19:23,793
I need your help.

416
00:19:25,712 --> 00:19:30,632
If it's not for me, for Amisha,
her husband and all the people killed.

417
00:19:30,633 --> 00:19:32,801
For Anna.

418
00:19:32,802 --> 00:19:35,763
That drive has the proof
connecting Sampson to all of this.

419
00:19:38,391 --> 00:19:40,517
I'll find the drive.

420
00:19:40,518 --> 00:19:42,603
When I see what's on it,

421
00:19:42,604 --> 00:19:44,647
I'll decide what to do with it.

422
00:19:45,899 --> 00:19:47,816
That's all I can ask. Thank you.

423
00:19:47,817 --> 00:19:50,445
[dramatic, suspenseful music playing]

424
00:19:55,450 --> 00:19:57,200
Don't thank me yet.

425
00:19:57,201 --> 00:19:58,785
Looks like the Big Red Machine found us.

426
00:19:58,786 --> 00:20:00,371
- How?
- You were followed.

427
00:20:01,289 --> 00:20:02,915
Might want to hire some new security.

428
00:20:02,916 --> 00:20:04,792
What are you gonna do?

429
00:20:07,420 --> 00:20:08,963
Make 'em regret walking in here.

430
00:20:10,381 --> 00:20:11,341
[man] Perhaps not.

431
00:20:14,510 --> 00:20:16,512
Guns on the table, or the girl dies.

432
00:20:18,097 --> 00:20:19,724
Now.

433
00:20:23,353 --> 00:20:25,604
Get up. We're taking a walk.

434
00:20:25,605 --> 00:20:27,564
Let's go. Sit down.

435
00:20:27,565 --> 00:20:29,275
You, sit down.

436
00:20:31,611 --> 00:20:36,698
[DJ over speaker] <i>It's time
for Midnight Cosmic Bowl!</i>

437
00:20:36,699 --> 00:20:39,451
<i>All strikes get a free pitcher
of Rolling Rock,</i>

438
00:20:39,452 --> 00:20:43,497
<i>and we got buy-one-get-one apps!</i>

439
00:20:43,498 --> 00:20:45,582
- [applause and cheering]
- [airhorn sounding]

440
00:20:45,583 --> 00:20:47,585
[♪ KC and the Sunshine band singing
"Boogie Shoes"]

441
00:20:52,924 --> 00:20:54,091
- Get them out of here.
- [screaming]

442
00:20:54,092 --> 00:20:58,804
<i>♪ I can't wait till I see you again</i>...

443
00:20:58,805 --> 00:21:01,182
[grunting]

444
00:21:03,101 --> 00:21:08,105
<i>♪ I want to on put on my, my, my, my,
my boogie shoes ♪</i>

445
00:21:08,106 --> 00:21:11,066
<i>♪ Just to boogie with you, yeah ♪</i>

446
00:21:11,067 --> 00:21:14,611
<i>♪ I want to put on my, my, my, my,
my boogie shoes...</i>

447
00:21:14,612 --> 00:21:16,905
[people screaming]

448
00:21:16,906 --> 00:21:19,116
[grunting]

449
00:21:19,117 --> 00:21:23,204
<i>♪ I want to do it till the sun comes up...</i>

450
00:21:24,288 --> 00:21:25,914
Come on.

451
00:21:25,915 --> 00:21:27,374
<i>♪ Oh, yeah ♪</i>

452
00:21:27,375 --> 00:21:31,044
<i>♪ I want to do it
till I can't get enough ♪</i>

453
00:21:31,045 --> 00:21:34,715
<i>♪ Yeah, yeah, uh-huh...</i>

454
00:21:34,716 --> 00:21:36,842
<i>♪ I want to put on...</i>

455
00:21:36,843 --> 00:21:40,555
[yelling]

456
00:21:42,515 --> 00:21:47,311
<i>♪ I want to put on my, my, my, my
my boogie shoes ♪</i>

457
00:21:48,646 --> 00:21:52,024
♪ ♪

458
00:21:52,025 --> 00:21:54,527
[grunting]

459
00:22:04,704 --> 00:22:07,123
- [panting]
- [wood clatters on ground]

460
00:22:08,958 --> 00:22:10,542
I told you to get out of here.

461
00:22:10,543 --> 00:22:12,377
The guards took Amisha to the hotel.

462
00:22:12,378 --> 00:22:14,088
I couldn't just leave you behind.

463
00:22:15,840 --> 00:22:17,340
Thank you.

464
00:22:17,341 --> 00:22:19,384
[grunts, exhales]

465
00:22:19,385 --> 00:22:21,344
[sirens wailing]

466
00:22:21,345 --> 00:22:24,347
Ah, we got to go.

467
00:22:24,348 --> 00:22:26,516
If I get caught in the system,
the CIA will find me.

468
00:22:26,517 --> 00:22:27,977
CIA?

469
00:22:29,020 --> 00:22:31,355
[indistinct radio chatter]

470
00:22:34,734 --> 00:22:36,152
[officer] Let 'em through.

471
00:22:51,793 --> 00:22:53,795
[insects trilling]

472
00:22:57,173 --> 00:22:59,716
- This is it?
- Yeah.

473
00:22:59,717 --> 00:23:02,845
Any idea why she'd ditch
a perfectly good car, take the subway?

474
00:23:03,888 --> 00:23:05,972
- None.
- Whoever took it

475
00:23:05,973 --> 00:23:07,641
knew exactly what they were doing.

476
00:23:07,642 --> 00:23:11,353
Take it to a secluded spot,
toss the hell out of it.

477
00:23:11,354 --> 00:23:13,146
Like Anna's house.

478
00:23:13,147 --> 00:23:15,023
CIA again?

479
00:23:15,024 --> 00:23:16,025
Maybe.

480
00:23:17,235 --> 00:23:19,986
Had to know
local cops were looking for this car.

481
00:23:19,987 --> 00:23:22,323
Didn't want to get caught
with it next to the subway.

482
00:23:23,282 --> 00:23:25,325
{\an8}Damn. Look at this shit.

483
00:23:25,326 --> 00:23:26,785
{\an8}[tense music playing]

484
00:23:26,786 --> 00:23:29,913
{\an8}Glove box forced open, seats shredded.

485
00:23:29,914 --> 00:23:31,374
Look at this mail.

486
00:23:33,793 --> 00:23:35,252
Credit card offer.

487
00:23:35,253 --> 00:23:37,504
"Act fast, get 5,000 extra points."

488
00:23:37,505 --> 00:23:40,215
If this was street hoods,
they would have taken this,

489
00:23:40,216 --> 00:23:41,633
run a little fraud.

490
00:23:41,634 --> 00:23:43,718
This was definitely
people looking for something.

491
00:23:43,719 --> 00:23:46,096
- The drive.
- [Tamara] Yeah.

492
00:23:46,097 --> 00:23:47,974
[slow, suspenseful music playing]

493
00:23:53,938 --> 00:23:55,606
Bear Creek pass.

494
00:23:56,941 --> 00:23:59,694
Never cleaned out her trunk
from last winter.

495
00:24:00,987 --> 00:24:03,196
So her winter jacket
would have been here, too.

496
00:24:03,197 --> 00:24:05,490
And you can't make a guess
as to why she would put it on

497
00:24:05,491 --> 00:24:06,826
and get on a hot subway?

498
00:24:08,327 --> 00:24:09,829
No idea.

499
00:24:11,706 --> 00:24:14,374
Well, looks like she just happened
to still have this stuff in the back.

500
00:24:14,375 --> 00:24:17,085
So if Anna left the house
without a coat,

501
00:24:17,086 --> 00:24:19,087
wearing one wasn't part of her plan.

502
00:24:19,088 --> 00:24:21,381
It became part of the plan

503
00:24:21,382 --> 00:24:23,133
after she got in the car.

504
00:24:23,134 --> 00:24:25,094
None of this is logical.

505
00:24:26,512 --> 00:24:27,805
Yeah.

506
00:24:31,392 --> 00:24:33,476
[melancholy music playing]

507
00:24:33,477 --> 00:24:35,021
You okay?

508
00:24:38,733 --> 00:24:40,443
I was supposed to go with her and Ben...

509
00:24:41,777 --> 00:24:42,904
...to Bear Creek...

510
00:24:44,155 --> 00:24:47,824
...but I canceled last minute
so I could...

511
00:24:47,825 --> 00:24:49,910
[laughs]

512
00:24:49,911 --> 00:24:52,580
...reinsulate my fucking garage.

513
00:24:55,291 --> 00:24:57,835
I am never gonna get
to do anything with them again.

514
00:25:00,838 --> 00:25:02,923
At least my garage is...

515
00:25:02,924 --> 00:25:04,257
warm, right?

516
00:25:04,258 --> 00:25:05,384
Hey.

517
00:25:06,552 --> 00:25:09,597
You know what kind of people put themself
through hell after a loved one dies?

518
00:25:10,932 --> 00:25:12,933
Like, shoulda, I-I coulda,

519
00:25:12,934 --> 00:25:14,352
I wish I had?

520
00:25:17,104 --> 00:25:19,190
Good people.

521
00:25:20,358 --> 00:25:22,360
But it doesn't help you...

522
00:25:24,403 --> 00:25:26,530
...and it's not gonna help finding
who did all this.

523
00:25:30,409 --> 00:25:31,953
So, you still with me?

524
00:25:32,954 --> 00:25:35,164
Head in the game?

525
00:25:40,419 --> 00:25:41,920
[sighs]

526
00:25:41,921 --> 00:25:43,421
All right.

527
00:25:43,422 --> 00:25:44,673
[Russell] No GPS.

528
00:25:44,674 --> 00:25:46,508
Phone mount on dash.

529
00:25:46,509 --> 00:25:49,095
Probably used her cell to navigate.

530
00:25:50,179 --> 00:25:54,057
There was no phone found on Anna at the
subway station, and nothing on the train.

531
00:25:54,058 --> 00:25:55,767
Well, whoever searched must have taken it.

532
00:25:55,768 --> 00:25:56,978
[Jacob] Move.

533
00:26:01,691 --> 00:26:03,817
It's a work badge.

534
00:26:03,818 --> 00:26:06,194
We found Anna's work badge at her house.

535
00:26:06,195 --> 00:26:08,863
Whole point of those things is security,
getting onto federal property.

536
00:26:08,864 --> 00:26:10,366
They don't give out extras.

537
00:26:11,450 --> 00:26:14,995
[inhales] Anna ever mention a second job
or working someplace new?

538
00:26:14,996 --> 00:26:17,330
- No. Never.
- [suspenseful music playing]

539
00:26:17,331 --> 00:26:19,041
[garbled radio transmission]

540
00:26:21,502 --> 00:26:24,505
Shit. Feds. We got to go.

541
00:26:26,382 --> 00:26:29,050
What are you doing? What?

542
00:26:29,051 --> 00:26:31,636
Listen, once you shoved that thing
in your pocket,

543
00:26:31,637 --> 00:26:33,430
you tainted chain of custody.

544
00:26:33,431 --> 00:26:35,974
If that badge leads to anything,
you'll never get a conviction.

545
00:26:35,975 --> 00:26:39,644
Federal government takes over a suicide.
CIA kidnaps us.

546
00:26:39,645 --> 00:26:41,855
The Reds try to kill us.

547
00:26:41,856 --> 00:26:44,150
Fuck the chain of custody.

548
00:26:45,067 --> 00:26:47,278
I want to find out
what my sister died over.

549
00:26:50,364 --> 00:26:52,366
[♪ Coco Montoya sings
"Lights Are On But Nobody's Home"]

550
00:26:54,869 --> 00:26:57,872
<i>♪ Playin with my mind, babe ♪</i>

551
00:27:01,876 --> 00:27:04,044
<i>♪ always accusin' me...</i>

552
00:27:04,045 --> 00:27:05,755
[groans]

553
00:27:07,048 --> 00:27:08,632
[knocking]

554
00:27:12,178 --> 00:27:14,346
<i>♪ I said,
you're playing with my mind ♪</i>

555
00:27:14,347 --> 00:27:15,765
- <i>♪ babe ♪</i>
- [knocking]

556
00:27:20,686 --> 00:27:22,730
<i>♪ always accusin' me ♪</i>

557
00:27:24,231 --> 00:27:26,399
- <i>♪ of doin' wrong...</i>
- New clothes.

558
00:27:26,400 --> 00:27:28,903
Guy was selling 'em outside the Way-Way
across the street.

559
00:27:30,654 --> 00:27:32,114
Wawa.

560
00:27:33,199 --> 00:27:34,949
How's Amisha holding up?

561
00:27:34,950 --> 00:27:37,535
She and the guards are in for the night.

562
00:27:37,536 --> 00:27:39,872
No injuries. They got away clean.

563
00:27:40,831 --> 00:27:43,708
Um, she's scared.

564
00:27:43,709 --> 00:27:45,502
People are trying to hurt her again.

565
00:27:45,503 --> 00:27:48,047
It's bringing back a lot of bad memories.

566
00:27:48,547 --> 00:27:50,007
<i>♪ Yeah ♪</i>

567
00:27:51,759 --> 00:27:53,885
<i>♪ Now here come my mother-in-law ♪</i>

568
00:27:53,886 --> 00:27:55,388
I travel light.

569
00:27:57,348 --> 00:27:59,015
<i>♪ It's the same thing every day...</i>

570
00:27:59,016 --> 00:28:00,434
You're hurt pretty bad.

571
00:28:02,019 --> 00:28:03,437
Been better.

572
00:28:04,480 --> 00:28:05,772
Been worse.

573
00:28:05,773 --> 00:28:06,816
Thank you.

574
00:28:07,900 --> 00:28:09,235
For giving me a second chance.

575
00:28:11,445 --> 00:28:13,572
Sorry I lied to you. I didn't want to.

576
00:28:14,657 --> 00:28:16,283
Yes, you did.

577
00:28:17,284 --> 00:28:18,952
That's why you said
a specific set of words

578
00:28:18,953 --> 00:28:20,955
in a specific way at a specific time.

579
00:28:21,914 --> 00:28:23,124
To tell a lie.

580
00:28:24,208 --> 00:28:25,708
But I've got a firm policy
to forgive liars

581
00:28:25,709 --> 00:28:28,212
who save me
from getting brained in an alley.

582
00:28:29,171 --> 00:28:30,630
<i>♪ I can see your lights...</i>

583
00:28:30,631 --> 00:28:32,091
Thank you.

584
00:28:33,926 --> 00:28:37,220
But I still wish
I didn't have to be deceptive.

585
00:28:37,221 --> 00:28:40,098
Getting to the truth,
getting the story out,

586
00:28:40,099 --> 00:28:42,476
it just required I not tell the truth.

587
00:28:43,894 --> 00:28:45,478
That was the cost.

588
00:28:45,479 --> 00:28:47,021
But is the cost worth it?

589
00:28:47,022 --> 00:28:51,443
We could've been killed back there
at that bowling alley or at the hotel.

590
00:28:51,444 --> 00:28:53,195
I don't run away from danger.

591
00:28:55,239 --> 00:28:56,823
You don't seem to, either.

592
00:28:56,824 --> 00:28:59,242
I'm a 250-pound former soldier.

593
00:28:59,243 --> 00:29:02,787
You're a 110-pound reporter,
and your security's average at best.

594
00:29:02,788 --> 00:29:04,789
Maybe you should take Amisha
and go back home.

595
00:29:04,790 --> 00:29:06,040
Never.

596
00:29:06,041 --> 00:29:10,086
What we're trying to do here,
it's bigger than just me and Amisha.

597
00:29:10,087 --> 00:29:11,296
And she agrees.

598
00:29:11,297 --> 00:29:13,173
We came here for information,
and the last person

599
00:29:13,174 --> 00:29:15,134
to have that information was in this city.

600
00:29:16,635 --> 00:29:18,303
So, no.

601
00:29:18,304 --> 00:29:19,596
We're not leaving.

602
00:29:19,597 --> 00:29:21,514
Even if you die as a result?

603
00:29:21,515 --> 00:29:24,768
I told you, I'm not a person who runs.

604
00:29:26,187 --> 00:29:27,897
What happened there?

605
00:29:30,149 --> 00:29:32,860
Guess I'm not a person who runs, either.

606
00:29:37,448 --> 00:29:39,158
Is that why you're still here?

607
00:29:44,705 --> 00:29:47,416
You feel bad
you couldn't help Anna, don't you?

608
00:29:48,501 --> 00:29:50,752
That poor woman looked you in the eyes,

609
00:29:50,753 --> 00:29:55,048
confused, petrified, broken,

610
00:29:55,049 --> 00:29:56,550
and you couldn't do anything.

611
00:29:59,345 --> 00:30:00,929
But hey,

612
00:30:00,930 --> 00:30:03,349
who do you think's more responsible?

613
00:30:05,059 --> 00:30:08,103
The man who said a few words
to Anna on a subway...

614
00:30:10,064 --> 00:30:12,024
...or the reporter who put her there?

615
00:30:17,821 --> 00:30:19,448
I'll get your back.

616
00:30:27,456 --> 00:30:31,210
My mother gave me this bracelet
when I was 16.

617
00:30:34,588 --> 00:30:37,758
She was a real journalist.

618
00:30:40,094 --> 00:30:42,346
Died in action in Indonesia.

619
00:30:46,183 --> 00:30:48,477
She's why I'm a reporter.

620
00:30:52,815 --> 00:30:54,608
This bracelet...

621
00:30:56,277 --> 00:30:58,445
...is all I have of her.

622
00:31:00,906 --> 00:31:02,740
Two seeds

623
00:31:02,741 --> 00:31:05,243
in each box,

624
00:31:05,244 --> 00:31:07,829
supposed to be...

625
00:31:07,830 --> 00:31:09,456
me and her...

626
00:31:11,959 --> 00:31:13,627
...always together.

627
00:31:16,672 --> 00:31:19,341
Each box a different stage of our lives.

628
00:31:23,554 --> 00:31:25,096
<i>♪ Accusin' me...</i>

629
00:31:25,097 --> 00:31:27,891
And I tell people
it's just a regular charm bracelet.

630
00:31:31,562 --> 00:31:33,314
For some reason...

631
00:31:35,399 --> 00:31:37,860
...it doesn't feel right
to lie to you again.

632
00:31:40,321 --> 00:31:43,032
<i>♪ Oh, but I can't see ♪</i>

633
00:31:44,116 --> 00:31:47,578
<i>♪ I can't see nobody home ♪</i>

634
00:31:48,746 --> 00:31:52,416
<i>♪ No, no, no ♪</i>

635
00:32:12,853 --> 00:32:15,648
Can't say I'm comfortable
doing this on my laptop.

636
00:32:16,649 --> 00:32:18,191
- I don't give a fuck.
- [chuckles]

637
00:32:18,192 --> 00:32:19,276
Yeah? Well, I do.

638
00:32:20,277 --> 00:32:22,528
Look, man, I may record CIA sites,

639
00:32:22,529 --> 00:32:24,989
use burners, live off the grid,
but all that's legal.

640
00:32:24,990 --> 00:32:27,450
Taking your sister's badge
from a crime scene?

641
00:32:27,451 --> 00:32:28,911
That's federal evidence tampering.

642
00:32:30,579 --> 00:32:32,288
Then leave.

643
00:32:32,289 --> 00:32:34,457
- You don't have to be here.
- [chuckles]

644
00:32:34,458 --> 00:32:37,044
Okay, yeah.
That's easy for you to say.

645
00:32:39,338 --> 00:32:42,674
You want to know why
I only write for online publications now?

646
00:32:42,675 --> 00:32:46,094
Hmm? Because, a year ago, I was working
a banking scandal that implicated

647
00:32:46,095 --> 00:32:49,013
the Fed and a lot of the powers that be,
including my own newspaper,

648
00:32:49,014 --> 00:32:52,267
who I discovered had been sitting
on the story for years.

649
00:32:53,602 --> 00:32:56,062
When the watchers essentially work
for the watched,

650
00:32:56,063 --> 00:32:58,022
yeah, you don't stand a chance.

651
00:32:58,023 --> 00:33:02,402
They not only fired me,
they discredited me, humiliated me,

652
00:33:02,403 --> 00:33:04,570
called me a crackpot,

653
00:33:04,571 --> 00:33:07,448
planted insane conspiracy theory documents
on my laptop,

654
00:33:07,449 --> 00:33:10,119
manipulated my expense reports,
make it look like that I was stealing.

655
00:33:11,120 --> 00:33:12,454
Made my own father doubt me.

656
00:33:14,998 --> 00:33:16,333
So, yeah...

657
00:33:18,001 --> 00:33:19,670
...I need to be here.

658
00:33:21,797 --> 00:33:24,215
Here. Knew we had one in evidence.

659
00:33:24,216 --> 00:33:27,385
Fraud raided a fake ID ring
a few weeks ago. It should work.

660
00:33:27,386 --> 00:33:28,636
Okay.

661
00:33:28,637 --> 00:33:29,763
[laptop beeps]

662
00:33:30,681 --> 00:33:31,806
Ready to go.

663
00:33:31,807 --> 00:33:34,643
[tense, dramatic music playing]

664
00:33:38,147 --> 00:33:39,772
[beeping]

665
00:33:39,773 --> 00:33:41,858
Let's see what we got here.

666
00:33:41,859 --> 00:33:44,026
- [beep]
- All right.

667
00:33:44,027 --> 00:33:46,571
Name: Anna Merrick.

668
00:33:46,572 --> 00:33:49,490
Work address and access allowance

669
00:33:49,491 --> 00:33:51,493
to Building 28, station J12.

670
00:33:53,912 --> 00:33:55,456
Level Eight clearance?

671
00:33:56,540 --> 00:33:58,207
Thought you said
your sister was an HR clerk?

672
00:33:58,208 --> 00:34:00,002
What the hell's she doing
with Level Eight clearance?

673
00:34:02,129 --> 00:34:04,088
I-I have no idea.

674
00:34:04,089 --> 00:34:06,592
Well, at least we know
where we're going to next.

675
00:34:07,634 --> 00:34:09,844
Tell me that badge isn't from the car.

676
00:34:09,845 --> 00:34:12,388
Forget what you think you see and leave.

677
00:34:12,389 --> 00:34:14,725
Well, I see stolen evidence.

678
00:34:16,643 --> 00:34:18,437
No. You don't.

679
00:34:19,646 --> 00:34:21,273
You're gonna intimidate me?

680
00:34:22,149 --> 00:34:24,109
I'm no fuckin' rat, Green.

681
00:34:25,110 --> 00:34:26,652
I'm no idiot.

682
00:34:26,653 --> 00:34:28,279
This is a federal case.

683
00:34:28,280 --> 00:34:32,743
I'm not you. I'm not content
to blow up my career over bullshit.

684
00:34:33,619 --> 00:34:35,579
So if they come to me, if they ask me...

685
00:34:37,331 --> 00:34:39,291
...I'm gonna tell 'em what I've seen.

686
00:34:42,085 --> 00:34:44,087
Go back to your desk, Shaun.

687
00:34:49,927 --> 00:34:52,346
All right, grab your shit. We got to move.

688
00:34:56,058 --> 00:34:58,769
You know you're way too big
to make a quiet getaway, right?

689
00:34:59,770 --> 00:35:00,978
Didn't want to wake you.

690
00:35:00,979 --> 00:35:04,690
Don't always sneak out in the middle of
the night, but I have a drive ahead of me.

691
00:35:04,691 --> 00:35:06,484
Where to?

692
00:35:06,485 --> 00:35:07,778
Baltimore.

693
00:35:08,612 --> 00:35:10,696
Want to get there
first thing in the morning.

694
00:35:10,697 --> 00:35:12,532
Gives me two and a half hours tops.

695
00:35:12,533 --> 00:35:14,033
What's there?

696
00:35:14,034 --> 00:35:15,785
Sampson.

697
00:35:15,786 --> 00:35:18,205
After what you told me,
there's more we need to discuss.

698
00:35:20,040 --> 00:35:22,125
Just come back safe, okay?

699
00:35:25,128 --> 00:35:27,965
I don't want anyone else
I dragged into this getting hurt.

700
00:35:32,719 --> 00:35:34,012
[entry bell chimes]

701
00:35:37,266 --> 00:35:39,268
[slow, dramatic music playing]

702
00:35:45,858 --> 00:35:47,943
What the hell was Anna doing here?

703
00:35:52,155 --> 00:35:54,240
[breathing heavily]

704
00:35:54,241 --> 00:35:56,243
♪ ♪

705
00:36:01,290 --> 00:36:03,292
[indistinct crowd chatter]

706
00:36:05,836 --> 00:36:07,504
[Reacher] Springfield.

707
00:36:09,006 --> 00:36:10,674
Here we go.

708
00:36:14,761 --> 00:36:18,932
Reacher. Congressman Sampson was wondering
when he'd see you again.

709
00:36:19,850 --> 00:36:20,934
Follow me.

710
00:36:25,105 --> 00:36:26,815
[elevator bell dings]

711
00:36:33,530 --> 00:36:35,156
- You coming?
- Last time

712
00:36:35,157 --> 00:36:38,201
you said I was going to meet Sampson,
I wound up talking to federal flunkies.

713
00:36:39,036 --> 00:36:40,913
How do I know
you're not gonna screw me again?

714
00:36:41,997 --> 00:36:43,540
Get on the elevator and find out.

715
00:36:50,339 --> 00:36:52,090
[elevator bell dings]

716
00:36:54,051 --> 00:36:57,970
Oh. Unless you want to be
bouncing off the walls, stop at half.

717
00:36:57,971 --> 00:37:00,015
Half it is.

718
00:37:01,016 --> 00:37:02,558
[knocking at door]

719
00:37:02,559 --> 00:37:04,727
Come in, Springfield.

720
00:37:04,728 --> 00:37:06,687
Shouldn't leave the door open.

721
00:37:06,688 --> 00:37:09,232
You called me from the elevator
20 seconds ago.

722
00:37:10,233 --> 00:37:11,734
Reacher. [clears throat]

723
00:37:11,735 --> 00:37:12,986
Good to see you again.

724
00:37:13,987 --> 00:37:15,655
Would you like breakfast?

725
00:37:15,656 --> 00:37:17,573
I never turn down a meal.

726
00:37:17,574 --> 00:37:18,742
Then sit.

727
00:37:20,744 --> 00:37:22,536
There's a poached egg and a cup of fruit.

728
00:37:22,537 --> 00:37:23,664
I haven't touched it.

729
00:37:25,248 --> 00:37:26,917
[Reacher] Thank you.

730
00:37:28,251 --> 00:37:30,336
I can only assume that you came down here

731
00:37:30,337 --> 00:37:32,964
because you feel
there's information you want,

732
00:37:32,965 --> 00:37:35,133
or you have information to share.

733
00:37:36,134 --> 00:37:38,010
Which one is it?

734
00:37:38,011 --> 00:37:39,804
Both.

735
00:37:39,805 --> 00:37:41,181
[Sampson] Then go ahead.

736
00:37:44,559 --> 00:37:47,980
I already told you.
We have no secrets between us.

737
00:37:50,023 --> 00:37:51,817
All right.

738
00:37:52,818 --> 00:37:55,611
I want to know about your involvement
with General Putra.

739
00:37:55,612 --> 00:37:59,240
He tortured and slaughtered a number
of political dissidents in Indonesia.

740
00:37:59,241 --> 00:38:02,159
Mm-hmm. And?

741
00:38:02,160 --> 00:38:04,997
And my source tells me that he was trained
by Delta Force, led by you.

742
00:38:06,873 --> 00:38:08,249
And by the way you're squirming,

743
00:38:08,250 --> 00:38:10,209
I'd say you know the general's name,
and you were involved

744
00:38:10,210 --> 00:38:11,586
- in his atrocities.
- John...

745
00:38:12,796 --> 00:38:14,255
You're awfully calm
for a man being accused

746
00:38:14,256 --> 00:38:15,756
of what could be war crimes.

747
00:38:15,757 --> 00:38:17,883
[John] Your source only got part
of the story right.

748
00:38:17,884 --> 00:38:21,137
Yes, I was in Indonesia
with Delta Force in 1998,

749
00:38:21,138 --> 00:38:22,805
and I met Putra.

750
00:38:22,806 --> 00:38:24,557
I didn't much like him.

751
00:38:24,558 --> 00:38:26,976
Our mission was top secret,
and it remains so.

752
00:38:26,977 --> 00:38:28,728
But what I can disclose is that

753
00:38:28,729 --> 00:38:31,773
I have never tortured or killed civilians.

754
00:38:32,899 --> 00:38:36,570
And I've never assisted
in torturing or killing civilians.

755
00:38:37,696 --> 00:38:40,740
If you're trying to get a rise out of me,
it won't work.

756
00:38:40,741 --> 00:38:42,700
Simply because I'm telling the truth.

757
00:38:42,701 --> 00:38:44,952
Why didn't you tell me all this
the first time we met?

758
00:38:44,953 --> 00:38:49,040
Do you typically offer up details
of your top secret military operations

759
00:38:49,041 --> 00:38:51,460
- when a stranger asks?
- Point taken.

760
00:38:53,170 --> 00:38:55,296
Is there anything you can tell me
that you're not?

761
00:38:55,297 --> 00:38:56,422
Nothing.

762
00:38:56,423 --> 00:38:57,840
What about your chief of staff?

763
00:38:57,841 --> 00:38:59,508
- Cahill?
- What about him?

764
00:38:59,509 --> 00:39:00,926
The night Anna Merrick killed herself,

765
00:39:00,927 --> 00:39:03,930
there were four witnesses on the train,
but only three were interviewed by police.

766
00:39:04,723 --> 00:39:07,141
One ran away. It was Cahill.

767
00:39:07,142 --> 00:39:08,143
[scoffs]

768
00:39:09,978 --> 00:39:11,771
Excuse my language, but that's bullshit.

769
00:39:11,772 --> 00:39:13,939
- I saw him.
- Cahill wasn't on that train.

770
00:39:13,940 --> 00:39:15,441
- He would've told me.
- [Reacher] Would he?

771
00:39:15,442 --> 00:39:17,985
Seems people have a habit
of leaving out important details

772
00:39:17,986 --> 00:39:19,195
from the discussion around here.

773
00:39:19,196 --> 00:39:21,072
- Not Nolan Cahill.
- [Reacher] Really?

774
00:39:21,073 --> 00:39:23,908
Where is he? We could go ask him together.

775
00:39:23,909 --> 00:39:25,701
Fine.

776
00:39:25,702 --> 00:39:26,912
Let's go.

777
00:39:28,330 --> 00:39:30,332
[birds singing]

778
00:39:31,374 --> 00:39:32,876
[Sampson] Nolan.

779
00:39:34,795 --> 00:39:36,421
We need to speak.

780
00:39:39,591 --> 00:39:41,717
[exhales]

781
00:39:41,718 --> 00:39:43,844
Things never stay hidden, do they?

782
00:39:43,845 --> 00:39:46,222
What does that mean?

783
00:39:46,223 --> 00:39:50,143
Watergate, Iran-Contra,
Hunter's fucking laptop.

784
00:39:50,977 --> 00:39:53,562
Whatever people try to hide,
it always comes out.

785
00:39:53,563 --> 00:39:56,357
Why were you on the subway
with Anna Merrick the night she died?

786
00:39:56,358 --> 00:39:58,776
[dramatic music playing]

787
00:39:58,777 --> 00:40:00,570
Did you take her jump drive?

788
00:40:03,031 --> 00:40:04,908
You would've made a good president.

789
00:40:07,744 --> 00:40:09,286
I'm sorry, John.

790
00:40:09,287 --> 00:40:10,789
[Sampson] Nolan.

791
00:40:11,873 --> 00:40:12,874
- Nolan. Nolan!
- No!

792
00:40:15,210 --> 00:40:16,585
[car alarm ringing]

793
00:40:16,586 --> 00:40:18,004
Oh, my God.

794
00:40:19,089 --> 00:40:21,800
This is Springfield.
I need a perimeter and paramedic.

795
00:40:29,432 --> 00:40:31,685
[♪ Pixies play "Where's My Mind"]

796
00:40:34,771 --> 00:40:38,608
<i>♪ With your feet on the air
and your head on the ground ♪</i>

797
00:40:42,028 --> 00:40:45,364
<i>♪ try this trick and spin it ♪</i>

798
